icial intelligence is that the  computer is making decisions that the  human doesn't make so for a lot of AI  algorithms you just say optimize for X  optimize for y so for example in credit  approvals you might say look at all the  data and optimize for a system that  minimizes defaults and then you don't  know what goes into that you're saying  some of these companies don't even know  how their own algorithms work they know  what the algorithms are trying to do  they don't know exactly  I think algorithm is getting there yeah  so you can try to audit it you can try  to break it apart you can look at  different factors take different inputs  but it is a complicated process it is  one of the most interesting questions of  our time how do we get justice in a  system where we don't know how the  algorithms are working well you could  argue for an algorithm because it's not  that old white bank manager making these  decision one guy behind a counter it's  now a machine and that doesn't have the  biases we do but but if the biases are  built-in because a programmer put them  there like your stuff or the data put  them there but one of the reasons why  when this story broke I was a little  skeptical is that probably the system is  set up to do what I said before right to  minimize defaults and a system that's  set to minimize defaults is not going to  give all men 20 times the credit limit  of all women perhaps it will have a tiny  difference based on historical data I  would expect maybe it would be  beneficial for women who are maybe more  responsible with their credit so this  story sounds like it probably the  outcome we're seeing is probably  connected to the very high income of the  tech founders tweeting about it so  doesn't it seem like there's a bigger  problem here than what it appears to be  I think this story is probably not the  best example of a huge issue all right  Nick Thompson thank you very much
 

